      "category": "engine",
      "title": "4.3L V6 Intake Manifold Gasket Leak",
      "description": "Same intake gasket issue as all GM 4.3L vehicles. The composite gaskets fail, allowing coolant leaks. The Astro version is particularly problematic due to the engine positioned under the dash, making repairs more labor-intensive.",
      "solution": "Pressure-test the cooling system and inspect the lower intake manifold area for external seepage, then confirm whether coolant is also entering the crankcase or intake ports by checking for milky oil, misfires, or unexplained coolant loss. Repair requires removing the doghouse/interior engine cover and upper intake components, then replacing the lower intake manifold gaskets with the updated metal-reinforced GM/Fel-Pro style set, along with new coolant, thermostat, and often the distributor gasket and heater hose quick-connect if disturbed. Carefully clean the sealing surfaces, check the intake manifold for warpage, and torque the bolts in the proper sequence to spec to prevent repeat leaks. Typical repair cost is about $700-$1,400 due to the Astro van's difficult access and 6-10 hours of labor.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "symptoms": [
        "Coolant loss",
        "Overheating",
        "Rough idle",
        "Sweet coolant smell"
      ],
      "affectedSystems": [],
      "dtcCodes": [
        "P0171",
        "P0174"
      ],
